---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { get } from "../lib/people.js";
const h = get("@I17@");
---
<Base
  title="Hedviga"
  eyebrow="Senj 1926 — Johannesburg 2001"
  dek="Hedviga “Seka” Blažević was born in a dying port on the Adriatic and died seventy-five years later on the Highveld, having crossed a world war, a country that stopped existing, and two continents.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">She is the reason this archive exists, and she is also its best-documented
    person: a birth at <strong>Senj on 3 August 1926</strong>, a marriage into the Defranceski
    family, three children, an <strong>immigration from Zimbabwe in April 1975</strong>, a second
    marriage to a Sephardic Jew from Rhodes, and a death at
    <strong>Johannesburg on 30 October 2001</strong> with the cause written down plainly.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>What the record gives</h3>
        <table class="kv">
          <tr><th>Born</th><td>3 August 1926, Senj</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Father</th><td><a href={u("/people/ljubomir-blazevic/")}>Ljubomir “Ljubo”
            Blažević</a> (1892–1966), <em>mornar</em> — sailor</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Mother</th><td><a href={u("/milka/")}>Milka Lucia Papić</a>
            (1886–1971) — tobacco-factory worker at twenty-one, widow at twenty-eight, supervisor by
            the end</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Siblings</th><td>Leo (1920–2008), Karmela “Carmen” (1922–1987),
            <a href={u("/oto/")}>Oto (1923–1945)</a>, Ljubomir (1929–2007), Mario (1933–1984),
            Anton</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Half-siblings</th><td>By her mother's first marriage:
            <a href={u("/people/miroslava-mica-nana-vukelic/")}>Miroslava “Mica” Vukelić</a>
            (1907–1993) and <a href={u("/people/josip-vukelic/")}>Josip Vukelić</a> (1913–2004).
            <a href={u("/milka/")}>Milka's two marriages →</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Married</th><td>Ivan Anton Defranceski (1925–1995) — who spent part of the war
            as a sixteen-year-old labourer in a German power works.
            <a href={u("/arolsen/")}>The Arolsen cards →</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Children</th><td>Ladislava “Ada” (b. 1948, Crikvenica), Ivan Anton (b. 1951,
            Senj), Robert “Robbie” (b. 1953, Senj)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Emigrated</th><td>April 1975, to South Africa <strong>from
            Zimbabwe</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Later married</th><td><a href={u("/rhodes/")}>Santou (Shemtov) Isaac Codron</a>
            (1913–1997), born on Rhodes — <em>c.</em> 1973, South Africa</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Died</th><td>30 October 2001, Johannesburg, aged 75</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Cause</th><td class="mono">Cancer of the rectum, spread to the liver</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The shape of the life</h3>
        <p>She was born into a household of <strong>seven children</strong> in a town whose economy
        had already collapsed — the railway had bypassed Senj half a century before she was born.
        Her mother supervised women rolling cigarettes; her father went to sea.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">She was <strong>fifteen</strong> when the Independent State of
        Croatia was declared and the war reached the coast, and <strong>nineteen</strong> when it
        ended and her brother Oto did not come home. Her first two children were born in
        <strong>Yugoslavia</strong>, at Crikvenica and Senj.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">Then Africa — and it is worth being exact about the order,
        because the record is: she was in <strong>Zimbabwe first</strong>, and came south to
        <strong>South Africa in April 1975</strong>. She is described as having married Codron
        around <strong>1973</strong>, which places that marriage in the Rhodesian years, not the
        South African ones.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Family lore, recorded September 2026</div>
    <h2>Twenty-two years this archive cannot account for</h2>
    <p class="lede">The record has her at <strong>Senj in April 1953</strong>, when Robert was born,
    and in <strong>South Africa in April 1975</strong>, arriving from Zimbabwe. Between those two
    dates there is <strong>nothing at all</strong> — twenty-two years, an emigration, and an entire
    country she is only known to have been in because of the words <em>“from Zimbabwe”</em> on the
    later entry.</p>

    <p>Three things are remembered in the family about that crossing. They are set down here as
    <strong>what is said</strong>, beside what the record holds, and the two do not entirely
    agree.</p>

    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <tr>
        <th>“They went in 1948”</th>
        <td><span class="tag">Family lore</span> Remembered as the year Ivan and Hedviga left.
          <strong>The record does not allow it.</strong> Their second and third children were born
          at <strong>Senj in December 1951 and April 1953</strong>. The family was in Croatia for
          at least five years after 1948.<br /><br />
          <strong>But 1948 is a real year in this family</strong>, and it may be what the memory is
          anchored to: <strong>Ladislava “Ada” was born at Crikvenica on 5 March 1948</strong>. A
          date attaching itself to the wrong event is the commonest thing in this archive — it is
          how <a href={u("/people/caetano-bacchi/")}>a man came to be born after his own
          granddaughter</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Why they went</th>
        <td><span class="tag">Family lore</span> A <strong>post-war migration</strong>, for better
          and safer conditions. <strong>Nothing in the record speaks to motive at all</strong> — not
          a single document here says why anyone left. What the record does supply is the setting:
          a town whose economy had collapsed seventy years earlier, a brother
          <a href={u("/oto/")}>dead in 1945</a> with no grave, and a husband who had spent the war
          from the age of sixteen as a <a href={u("/arolsen/")}>labourer in a German power
          works</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>She sang at the<br />Hotel Therapia</th>
        <td><span class="tag">Family lore</span> Remembered as a <strong>singer</strong> at the
          <strong>Hotel Therapia in Crikvenica</strong> — her husband's home town, and where their
          first child was born in 1948.<br /><br />
          <strong>The tree records no occupation for her whatsoever.</strong> Her entire event list
          is three lines: born, immigrated, died. Set against her mother — entered
          <em>nadglednica u tvornici duhana</em>, a <a href={u("/milka/")}>supervisor in the tobacco
          factory</a> — and her grandmother, a <em>nadničarka</em>, that silence is its own kind of
          record. <strong>This is the only thing anyone has ever said about what Hedviga did.</strong>
          <br /><br />
          <strong>And the venue is checkable, which lore rarely is.</strong> The Therapia opened in
          <strong>1895</strong> as the <em>Nadvojvoda Josip</em> — Archduke Joseph — with 120 beds,
          and was renamed <strong>Hotel Therapia in 1899</strong> when a hydropathic institute was
          established inside it. With the Miramare of 1906 it is the building that turned Crikvenica
          from a village into an Austro-Hungarian <strong>health resort</strong>. A woman singing
          there was singing in the grandest room on that stretch of coast.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>“I believe it closed down”</th>
        <td><span class="tag">Corrected</span> <strong>It did not.</strong> The building stands and
          is still a hotel. It was bought in <strong>2013</strong> by the Austrian hotelier
          <strong>Wilfried Holleis</strong>, renovated, and <strong>renamed Kvarner Palace</strong>
          — four stars, 92 rooms. What closed was <em>the name</em>, not the house.
          <br /><br />
          Which means the room she is remembered singing in is <strong>still there and can be
          walked into</strong>, and that is a rarer thing than a document.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Why this is written down rather than
    checked.</strong> A migration leaves paper — a passenger list, a permit, a naturalisation file,
    a school enrolment for three children. <strong>None of it has been looked for</strong>, because
    until now this archive did not know there was a Rhodesian decade to look in.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What would find it →</a></p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Two dates that fall on the same day</div>
    <h2>30 October</h2>
    <p class="lede">Her husband <strong>Ivan Anton Defranceski died at Senj on 30 October
    1995</strong>. Hedviga died at <strong>Johannesburg on 30 October 2001</strong> — six years
    later, to the day.</p>
    <p>This archive records the coincidence and claims nothing by it. Both dates come from the
    family tree rather than from a certificate seen here, and a shared date is exactly the kind of
    detail that gets tidied into place by memory. <strong>Two death certificates would settle
    it</strong>, and they are the first documents on the wanted list.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Recovered from MyHeritage, September 2026</div>
    <h2>Six pictures</h2>
    <p class="lede">None of these are in the GEDCOM — MyHeritage does not export media — so the
    first build of this archive had no face to put to any of it. They are
    <strong>480-pixel renderings</strong>, not the originals; the CDN signs each URL against its
    size, so a bigger one cannot simply be asked for.</p>
    <div class="photos">
      <figure>
        <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-06-500804.jpg")} loading="lazy"
             alt="Portrait of a woman in a bold floral print raising a glass, late 1960s" />
        <figcaption><strong>Almost certainly Hedviga</strong>, <em>c.</em> late 1960s — the best
        likeness in the set, and the same face as the 1980s group below.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-03-504173.jpg")} loading="lazy"
             alt="A family of five on a seafront balustrade, early 1950s" />
        <figcaption><strong>Very likely Ivan and Hedviga with their children</strong>, <em>c.</em>
        1953–54 — a man in a suit holding a baby in a bonnet, a girl in a bolero, a small child in
        a beret. The ages fit Ladislava, Ivan and the infant Robert.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-05-500852-granny-heddies-photos-25.jpg")} loading="lazy"
             alt="Colour family photograph, 1980s, with television and video recorder" />
        <figcaption>Captioned <em>“Granny Heddies Photos-25”</em>. Colour, <em>c.</em> 1980s, a
        television and a video recorder behind — this is Africa, not Senj.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-01-504176.jpg")} loading="lazy"
             alt="Multi-generational family group in a garden, 1950s" />
        <figcaption>Eleven people in a garden, <em>c.</em> 1950s. The two older women seated at the
        centre are in black — widows' mourning. Nobody in it is identified.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-02-504174.jpg")} loading="lazy"
             alt="Five people and a dog outdoors beside a truck, late 1950s" />
        <figcaption>Five people and a dog, <em>c.</em> late 1950s — a boy on a bicycle, a man in a
        singlet, a truck behind. Unidentified.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <a href={u("/certificate/")}>
          <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-04-501105-ivan-birth-certificate-translated.jpg")}
               loading="lazy" alt="Sworn translation of the 1951 Yugoslav birth extract" /></a>
        <figcaption><strong>Not a photograph.</strong> The sworn translation of her son's 1951
        Yugoslav birth extract — and the one document here that confirms her own birth from outside
        the family tree. <a href={u("/certificate/")}>Read it →</a></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><strong>Four of these five photographs are unidentified.</strong> The tree
    carries them as filenames — <em>1595624858.9990501</em>, <em>1134835622</em> — with no names,
    dates or places attached. Anyone who can put a name to a face in them should say so; that is the
    single most useful thing that could happen to this page.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the tree carries on her</h2>
    {h && h.notes && h.notes.length > 0 && (
      <div class="note">
        <p>The notes attached to her record are <strong>machine-written</strong> — a SmartCopy
        reference from Geni, and a MyHeritage record link. There is no biography in her own words.
        The <strong>six media items</strong> attached to her profile were absent from the GEDCOM
        export entirely; they have now been recovered and are shown above.</p>
      </div>
    )}
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Her record cites two independent trees — the
    <em>Defranceski Family Site</em> and the <em>Boras Web Site</em> — which is corroboration
    between witnesses, not between documents. <a href={u("/method/")}>Why that distinction is kept
    →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
